Here's the approach: bypass `IFormFile` (which buffers in memory) and read the multipart body as a stream, writing chunks directly to disk.

**1. Disable request size limits**

```csharp
// Program.cs
builder.WebApplication.WebHost.ConfigureKestrel(o =>
{
    o.Limits.MaxRequestBodySize = 2L * 1024 * 1024 * 1024; // 2 GB
});
```

**2. Stream the multipart sections to disk**

```csharp
app.MapPost("/upload", async (HttpRequest request) =>
{
    if (!request.HasFormContentType)
        return Results.BadRequest("Expected a multipart request.");

    var boundary = request.GetMultipartBoundary();
    if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(boundary))
        return Results.BadRequest("Missing boundary.");

    var reader = new MultipartReader(boundary, request.Body);
    MultipartSection? section;

    while ((section = await reader.ReadNextSectionAsync()) != null)
    {
        if (!ContentDispositionHeaderValue.TryParse(
                section.ContentDisposition, out var cd) ||
            !cd.IsFileDisposition())
            continue;

        var fileName = Path.GetRandomFileName(); // safe name
        var savePath = Path.Combine("/tmp/uploads", fileName);

        await using var fs = new FileStream(
            savePath, FileMode.Create, FileAccess.Write,
            FileShare.None, bufferSize: 81920);

        await section.Body.CopyToAsync(fs);

        return Results.Ok(new { saved = fileName });
    }

    return Results.BadRequest("No file found in request.");
})
.DisableAntiforgery();          // required for minimal-API file endpoints
```

**3. Required usings**

```csharp
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.WebUtilities; // MultipartReader
using Microsoft.Net.Http.Headers;        // ContentDispositionHeaderValue
```

**Key points:**

| Problem | Fix |
|---|---|
| `IFormFile` buffers entire body in RAM | `MultipartReader` streams section-by-section |
| Kestrel rejects bodies > 30 MB | Set `MaxRequestBodySize` to 2 GB |
| `[FromForm]` model binding also buffers | Read `request.Body` manually |
| `CopyToAsync` default 80 KB buffer | Uses ~80 KB RAM regardless of file size |

The `MultipartReader` reads the raw request stream one chunk at a time, so memory usage stays flat even for 2 GB files.
